                <h2>第一步：建立账户资产台账</h2>
<p>多账户管理的第一步不是给账户简单编号，而是建立统一台账。建议至少记录：广告账户ID、平台与主体、投放国家或地区、对应产品、账户角色、当前余额、日均消耗、目标获客成本、创建时间、代理关系及登录负责人。</p>
<p>账户命名可采用“区域-产品-角色-序号”格式，例如“US-工具App-主力-01”。名称应与实际用途一致，避免出现“备用”“测试2”等模糊标签。台账由投手每日更新，财务每周核对余额与消耗，负责人每月检查账户状态。</p>
<h2>第二步：按业务价值和风险分级</h2>
<p>可将账户分为三层：</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>核心层</strong>：承载主要收入、核心地区或高价值广告活动，需要优先保障余额、审核进度和素材迭代。</li>
<li><strong>增长层</strong>：用于新市场验证、扩量测试或中低客单价产品，允许一定试错，但应设置明确止损线。</li>
<li><strong>观察层</strong>：包含停用、测试、低消耗或存在合规疑问的账户，限制新增操作并定期复核。</li>
</ul>
<p>分级不能只看消耗量，还要结合账户稳定性、收入贡献、违规记录、充值依赖度和数据回传能力。例如某工具类App测试新市场时，将六个广告账户全部划为增长层，每个账户设置独立预算与停止条件；当其中两个账户连续产生稳定转化后，再升级为核心层。</p>
<h2>第三步：配置权限与操作边界</h2>
<p>按岗位分配权限：投手负责素材和日常优化，负责人审批预算调整，财务处理充值与对账，代理商只能访问被授权账户。高风险操作应设置二次确认，包括修改付款方式、调整账户上限、切换主体、删除广告系列以及更改收款信息。</p>
<p>建议每个账户设置一名主负责人和一名备用负责人，禁止多人共用单一登录凭证。离职、转岗或项目结束后，应在当天完成权限回收；长期未使用账户则退出登录并关闭自动续费或默认付款功能。</p>
<h2>第四步：分配预算并建立预警线</h2>
<p>预算应从核心层向增长层逐级释放，而不是平均分给所有账户。核心层可预留稳定额度，增长层采用小规模测试，观察层原则上不追加预算。</p>
<p>余额管理可设置三级预警：预计剩余3天时提醒负责人，预计剩余1天时通知财务，低于安全金额时暂停新增广告活动。计算公式可统一为：</p>
<blockquote>
<p>安全余额 = 日均消耗 × 充值到账周期 + 波动缓冲</p>
</blockquote>
<p>某跨境电商团队曾因多个账户同时消耗导致余额不足。调整后按账户角色建立日消耗上限，并让财务预留整体资金缓冲，临时停投情况明显减少。</p>
<h2>第五步：执行异常账户处理流程</h2>
<p>发现审核拒审、消耗突增、转化骤降、登录异常或付款失败时，先暂停风险操作，再截图并记录账户、素材、时间和影响范围。常见原因包括同一素材重复提交、落地页失效、支付方式异常或追踪链路中断。</p>
<p>处理完成后，应标注异常类型、处理人和恢复条件。涉及主体资质、付款信息或政策争议的账户，不应由投手自行解决，应及时转交平台客户经理及合规负责人。</p>
<h2>第六步：按周期复盘并升降级</h2>
<p>每周检查预算消耗、转化成本、余额和账户健康状态；每月重新评估账户层级；每个季度清理停用账户、失效素材和长期无消耗项目。升降级应保留记录，不因单日波动频繁调整。</p>
<p>每次复盘至少回答四个问题：预算是否投在正确的账户层级，账户是否出现规则性消耗异常，充值是否覆盖实际消耗，负责人和权限是否仍然匹配。最终目标是让每一笔预算、每一次操作和每一笔充值都能追溯到具体账户及责任人。</p>
            </div>
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Step 1: Build an Account Asset Register</h2>
<p>The first step in multi-account management is not simply assigning numbers. Create a shared register that records the advertising account ID, platform and entity, target country or region, product, account role, current balance, average daily spend, target acquisition cost, creation date, agency relationship, and account owner.</p>
<p>Use a consistent naming structure such as “Region-Product-Role-Number,” for example, “US-ToolApp-Core-01.” The name should reflect the actual purpose and avoid vague labels such as “backup” or “test 2.” Account managers should update the register daily, finance should reconcile balances and spending weekly, and managers should review account status monthly.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Tier Accounts by Business Value and Risk</h2>
<p>Accounts can be divided into three tiers:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Core tier:</strong> Accounts responsible for major revenue, priority regions, or high-value campaigns. These accounts should receive priority support for funding, review progress, and creative refreshes.</li>
<li><strong>Growth tier:</strong> Accounts used for new-market validation, scaling tests, or products with lower order values. Controlled experimentation is allowed, but clear stop-loss rules must be in place.</li>
<li><strong>Monitoring tier:</strong> Inactive, testing, low-spend, or compliance-flagged accounts. Limit new actions and conduct regular reviews.</li>
</ul>
<p>Tiering should not rely on spending alone. Consider account stability, revenue contribution, violation history, dependence on manual top-ups, and conversion-data reliability. For example, when a utility app tests a new market, all six accounts can initially be placed in the growth tier with separate budgets and stop conditions. Once two accounts produce stable conversions, they may be upgraded to the core tier.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Configure Permissions and Operational Boundaries</h2>
<p>Assign permissions by role. Media buyers manage creatives and daily optimization; managers approve budget changes; finance handles funding and reconciliation; agencies can only access authorized accounts. High-risk actions should require secondary confirmation, including changing payment methods, raising spending limits, switching entities, deleting campaigns, and modifying payout information.</p>
<p>Each account should have one primary owner and one backup. Do not share a single login across the team. When an employee leaves, changes roles, or completes a project, revoke access on the same day. For inactive accounts, log out of unused sessions and disable automatic renewals or default payment methods where possible.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Allocate Budgets and Set Balance Alerts</h2>
<p>Release budgets progressively from core to growth accounts rather than distributing funds evenly. Reserve stable funding for core accounts, use limited test budgets for growth accounts, and generally avoid adding funds to monitoring-tier accounts.</p>
<p>A three-level alert system can be used:</p>
<ul>
<li>Notify the owner when the balance is projected to last three days.</li>
<li>Notify finance when it is projected to last one day.</li>
<li>Pause new campaign activation when the balance falls below the safety threshold.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use a consistent calculation:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Safety balance = average daily spend × funding lead time + volatility buffer</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team once faced insufficient balances because several accounts consumed funds simultaneously. After introducing role-based daily spending caps and reserving an overall funding buffer for finance, emergency pauses were reduced.</p>
<h2>Step 5: Follow an Account Incident Procedure</h2>
<p>If an account experiences rejection, abnormal spending, a sudden conversion decline, suspicious login activity, or payment failure, first pause risky actions. Capture screenshots and document the account, creative, time, and scope of impact. Common causes include repeated creative submissions, broken landing pages, payment issues, or interrupted conversion tracking.</p>
<p>After resolution, label the incident type, owner, and conditions for restoring activity. Issues involving business credentials, payment information, or policy disputes should be escalated to the platform representative and compliance owner rather than handled independently by media buyers.</p>
<h2>Step 6: Review and Adjust Account Tiers</h2>
<p>Review spend, conversion cost, balance, and account health weekly; reassess account tiers monthly; and remove inactive accounts, expired creatives, and projects with no spending each quarter. Keep a record of every tier change and avoid adjusting tiers based on a single day of volatility.</p>
<p>Each review should answer four questions: Is the budget allocated to the right account tier? Does any account show recurring abnormal spending? Does funding cover actual consumption? Do account ownership and permissions remain appropriate? The goal is to make every budget decision, account action, and recharge fully traceable to a specific account and responsible person.</p>
